permalink: /en/entia/
lang: en
translation:
  lang: it
  url: /entia/
---

{# /en/entia/ — la specifica normativa inglese (R14).
   Non e' la traduzione di /entia/: tiene il normativo e lascia alla pagina
   italiana il racconto d'origine, le misure su questo archivio e la mappa.
   L'argomento e' la conformita' stessa: struttura fissa, vocabolario e
   parametri locali. Le distribuzioni di Claudio sono la prova che ENTIA e'
   usata qui, non una parte di cio' che un altro deve implementare.
   Testi: src/_data/entiaEn.js. Dati strutturali: src/_data/entia.js. #}

<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  "@id": "https://claudiocammarano.com/en/entia/#spec",
  "name": "ENTIA — {{ entia.sciolto }}",
  "headline": "ENTIA — normative specification",
  "version": "{{ entia.spec }}",
  "dateModified": "{{ entia.rilievi.data }}",
  "inLanguage": "en",
  "license": "{{ entia.licenza.url }}",
  "url": "https://claudiocammarano.com/en/entia/",
  "identifier": "https://doi.org/{{ entia.deposito.doi }}",
  "isBasedOn": "https://claudiocammarano.com/entia/",
  "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Claudio Cammarano", "@id": "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-3690-7466" }
}
</script>

{% include "components/entia-style.njk" %}

<div class="notazione-page">

  <header class="tag-page-header">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Navigation">
      <a href="/en/">Home</a>
      <span class="breadcrumb-sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <a href="/en/colophon/">Colophon</a>
      <span class="breadcrumb-sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <span class="breadcrumb-current">ENTIA</span>
    </nav>
    <p class="tag-page-label">Specification · version {{ entia.spec }}</p>
    <h1 class="tag-page-title">ENTIA</h1>
    <p class="tag-page-intro"><em>{{ entia.sciolto }}</em> — an open editorial notation for curated archives. {{ entiaEn.orientamento.normativo }}</p>
  </header>

  <div class="article-body">

    <dl class="notazione-meta">
      <div><dt>Version</dt><dd>{{ entia.spec }}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Deposited</dt><dd><a href="{{ entia.deposito.url }}">{{ entia.deposito.doi }}</a></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Licence</dt><dd><a href="{{ entia.licenza.url }}" rel="license">{{ entia.licenza.nome }}</a></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Data</dt><dd><a href="/entia.json">entia.json</a></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Italian text</dt><dd><a href="/entia/" lang="it" hreflang="it">/entia/</a></dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
    {%- for p in entiaEn.orientamento.cose %}
    <p>{{ p | safe }}</p>
    {%- endfor %}

    <h2 id="who">Who it is for</h2>
    {%- for p in entiaEn.orientamento.chi %}
    <p>{{ p | safe }}</p>
    {%- endfor %}

    <h2 id="name">The name</h2>
    <p>{{ entiaEn.orientamento.nome | safe }}</p>

    <h2 id="layers">The five layers</h2>
    <p>The notation is not a taxonomy of subjects: it is a scale of commitment. Each layer says the same thing about a text with a different degree of exposure to error, and is defined by what it <em>cannot</em> do.</p>

    <div class="entia-tab">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What binds it</th><th>What it asserts</th><th>What it cannot do</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
        {%- for s in entia.strati %}
          {%- set t = entiaEn.stratiTesti[s.nome] %}
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{{ entiaEn.strati[s.nome] }}</strong></td>
            <td>{{ t.lega }}</td>
            <td>{{ t.afferma }}</td>
            <td>{{ t.nonPuo }}</td>
          </tr>
        {%- endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    {%- for s in entia.strati %}
    <p class="entia-nota"><strong>{{ entiaEn.strati[s.nome] }}.</strong> {{ entiaEn.stratiTesti[s.nome].nota }}</p>
    {%- endfor %}

    <p class="entia-nota">The counts per layer are a property of this archive, not of the notation, and they are on the <a href="/entia/#strati" lang="it" hreflang="it">Italian page</a> with the date they were taken.</p>

    <h2 id="functions">The six functions</h2>
    <p>Every card declares one function only: what that reading did for this archive. When two both apply, <strong>the more specific one wins</strong>, in the order below. The order is normative; the names are not — see <a href="#conformance">Adopting ENTIA</a>.</p>

    {%- for f in entia.funzioni %}
    {%- set t = entiaEn.funzioniTesti[f.slug] %}
    <div class="entia-fn" id="{{ f.slug }}">
      <p class="ord">Precedence {{ f.precedenza }}</p>
      <h3>{{ entiaEn.funzioni[f.slug] }}</h3>
      <p>{{ t.estesa }}</p>
      <p class="prova">Test: {{ t.prova }}</p>
    </div>
    {%- endfor %}

    <h2 id="rules">The rules</h2>
    {%- for r in entia.regole %}
    {%- set rt = entiaEn.regole[r.nome] %}
    <div class="entia-regola"><b>{{ rt.nome }}</b>{{ rt.testo }}</div>
    {%- endfor %}

    <h2 id="scope">What the model is for</h2>
    <p>{{ entiaEn.limiti.premessa | safe }}</p>
    <dl class="entia-limiti">
      {%- for l in entiaEn.limiti.voci %}
      <dt>{{ l.titolo }}</dt>
      <dd>{{ l.testo | safe }}</dd>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>

    <h2 id="conformance">{{ entiaEn.conformita.titolo }}</h2>
    <p>{{ entiaEn.conformita.licenzaVsUso | safe }}</p>

    <h3>{{ entiaEn.conformita.struttura.titolo }}</h3>
    <p>{{ entiaEn.conformita.struttura.fisso | safe }}</p>
    <p>{{ entiaEn.conformita.struttura.locale | safe }}</p>

    <h3>{{ entiaEn.conformita.peso.titolo }}</h3>
    <p>{{ entiaEn.conformita.peso.testo | safe }}</p>
    <p>{{ entiaEn.conformita.peso.soglia | safe }}</p>

    <h3>The four conditions</h3>
    <p>{{ entiaEn.conformita.premessa }}</p>
    <ol>
      {%- for r in entiaEn.conformita.requisiti %}
      <li>{{ r }}</li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ol>

    <h2 id="deposit">The deposit, and how to cite it</h2>
    <p>The specification, the vocabulary in SKOS, the corpus with its assignments and its two dates, the map and the figures are deposited on Zenodo under {{ entia.licenza.nome }}: a public archive, independent of this site, which guarantees that version {{ entia.spec }} stays readable and unchanged even once this page has moved on.</p>
    <p class="entia-citazione">{{ entia.deposito.citazione }} <a href="{{ entia.deposito.url }}">{{ entia.deposito.doi }}</a></p>
    <p class="entia-nota">That DOI is the <em>concept</em> DOI: it always resolves to the latest version. Version {{ entia.spec }} also has a DOI of its own, <a href="https://doi.org/{{ entia.deposito.versionDoi }}">{{ entia.deposito.versionDoi }}</a>, for when you need to cite exactly this one and not a later one. For machines, the same specification is in <a href="/entia.json">/entia.json</a>, where the labels of the layers, the functions and the themes carry both languages. One caveat if you follow the DOI: the package deposited as {{ entia.spec }} carries the specification in Italian only. This English text and the bilingual vocabulary ship with the deposit of 1.1.</p>

    <h2 id="licence">Licence</h2>
    <p><a href="{{ entia.licenza.url }}" rel="license">{{ entia.licenza.nome }}</a>. Deliberately not the licence of the essays: NC-ND on a protocol would block the adoption that makes it useful, and ND would forbid the derived versions that are the sign a method is alive.</p>

  </div>
</div>
